course: dictionary
lesson: 8150
title: Request: asking politely
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A request is when you ask for something politely or formally. «Saying please turns an order into a polite request.» An order tells someone what to do. A request asks them, and they are free to say yes or no.
explain: Request can also be a verb. To request means to ask for something politely or officially. In «A petition is a written request signed by many people», request means the thing that was asked for, written down and signed by lots of people.
explain: Look for clues around the word. Words like «please», «could I…?» and «may I…?» show that a request is being made. Online, a «friend request» asks to connect with you, and you should only accept it from someone you know in real life.
fact: A request is the act of asking politely or formally for something.
fact: A request can also be the thing you asked for, like «Play my request!»
fact: To request means to ask for something politely or officially.
fact: Useful phrases include «on request», «at someone's request», «make a request» and «friend request».
fact: «Warning signs of a scam message are hurry, a secret, a request for money, codes or passwords.» Never share a password just because someone asks for it.
exercise: «Saying please turns an order into a polite request.» What is a request? | An order that must be obeyed | *The act of asking politely | A kind of game | A loud shout for help
exercise: What does a «friend request» do? | Gives someone money | Ends a friendship | Makes you wear a friend's clothes | *Asks to connect with someone online
exercise: Copy the one word that means to ask for something politely. | secret | hurry | *request | scam
test: «A petition is a written request signed by many people.» What does request mean here? | *Something asked for, written down | Giving a prize away | Running very fast | A type of boat
test: Which is a warning sign of a scam message? | A message from your mum saying hello | *A request for your passwords or codes | A note saying good morning | A kind teacher saying please
exam: Which sentence is a polite request? | Stop talking immediately! | Give me that now! | *Could I borrow your pencil, please? | Hand over your password.
